<font size=5>Search for Available Background Data</font><br>
<b>UI Eco-Hydraulics Group (UI)</b>
<p>
Using information supplied by Zane Fulbright (Archeologist, Red River Ranger District), Steve Clayton went to the BLM state office in Boise and found plats and homestead entry surveys for five properties in the meadow.  One of the surveys was completed in 1909, three in 1910, and one in 1919.  These survey notes contain interesting information about the meadow that allows us to supplement the existing knowledge base of historic conditions in the area.  Steve Clayton will share a summary of these notes at the Nov. 10th TAC meeting. 
 
<hr size="2" color=<%=color1%>>

<font size=5>Gibler Floodplain Survey</font><br>
<b>UI Eco-Hydraulics Group (UI)</b>
<p>
CE Engineering from Grangeville surveyed the meadow portion of the Gibler property with a GPS 
unit during the weekend of Oct. 9th - 11th.  The UI team provided CE Engineering with instructions for conducting the survey.  The survey will serve two purposes:  1) to collect upstream channel data for modeling flows through the entire meadow, and 2) to prepare a rough topographic map for the Giblers and project use. -

	</td>
	<td width="50%" valign="top">
	
<font size=5>September Monitoring</font><br>
<b>Steve Bauer</b><br>
<i>Pocket Water, Inc. (PWI)</i>
<p>
On September 21st - 22nd, Steve returned to finalize 1998 field work.  He completed the photo-point series, serviced turbidity equipment, and downloaded discharge/turbidity data.  He also took underwater photographs to document rock control sill condition and habitat feature development.  The picture quality may be limited by the turbidity level at the time.   Temperature data loggers were retrieved for the season. 

<hr size="2" color=<%=color1%>>

<font size=5>October Monitoring</font><br>
<b>UI Eco-Hydraulics Group (UI) and Steve Bauer (PWI)</b>
<p>
During the weekend of October 9th - 11th, 18 graduate and senior undergraduate students from the UI Civil Engineering and Mechanical Engineering departments surveyed channel cross sections throughout the meadow.  UI students and faculty familiar with the site and the study supervised the teams.  The information collected from these surveys will become part of our GIS database and used in project modeling and monitoring and evaluation.
<p>
Denny Dawes, Project Manager, and PWI participated in a project presentation for the students. 
They presented a general overview of the project and explained the relationship of the design to the development of micro-habitat features for chinook salmon.   
<p>
During this same weekend, PWI made some final stream discharge and groundwater elevation measurements, representative of the low flow conditions.  Turbidity and flow gauge data were downloaded and equipment dismantled for the season.  During the remainder of the fall period, PWI, UI, and WHI will be compiling and analyzing data from the 1998 field season. 

